{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1463 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c1b29e127a7eaec887b2d074d802429855e0a78eb5b891942b28e92bf264f1cb" :edb/db-before-t 1462 :edb/db-after-t 1463 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534775 50 #inst "2026-10-12T12:07:28.040Z" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186121778 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121782 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186124511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435676 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186124863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186124863 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5 \\cdot 5$ matrix and\n$Null(A) = {\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 3x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{1} \\\\ -2x_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\mid x_{1} ∈ R}$?\n\n1. $dim(Null(A)) = 0$\n2. $A$ is not invertible\n3. $rank(A) = 5$" 13194139534775 17592186045417 false] [17592186124863 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186124863 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186272239 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" 13194139534775 17592186045417 false] [17592186272239 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186272239 1074 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186272239 1074 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534775 17592186045417 false] [17592186435669 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435677 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435678 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435679 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1046 #edb/ref 17592186435679 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049340 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435669 1053 #uuid "26e5acff-8db9-5e5d-9122-36baf139baac" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435670 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435670 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435670 1083 #uuid "293235d4-a2f2-5777-a667-1540f003765f" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435671 1080 "I only" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435671 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435671 1083 #uuid "46090913-fe8a-53d8-a2e2-1edec9407dcb" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435672 1080 "II only" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435672 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435672 1083 #uuid "596e4b3c-1fd0-573a-82a9-d1617135d478" 13194139534775 17592186045417 true] [17592186435673 1080 "I and II 